        "title": "19th-Century Analysis - Cauchy, Riemann, Weierstrass, Cantor, Dedekind",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "By 1800, calculus was a century old and extraordinarily powerful. But it rested on\nshaky foundations. Newton's \"fluxions\" and Leibniz's \"infinitesimals\" were criticized\nby Berkeley as \"ghosts of departed quantities\" — you use them in calculations but\nthen discard them. Nobody had a precise definition of what a limit, a derivative,\nor an integral actually *was*."
    },
